==== WHAT THIS COMMITS YOU TO ====
  The min column mixes two clocks and now says which on every row. WALL is a
  figure whose own plan charges compiles and allocation. KERNEL is one whose
  plan says, in those words, that it does not. ALLOW is this file's own
  allowance for an arm whose plan prints no time estimate at all. FREE is an
  arm booked zero, whose plan refuses or times nothing, and which therefore
  has no clock to be on.
  
  thermal             ~  3 min WALL    starts at ~0 min          WHETHER THIS RENTED CARD CAN HOLD A CLOCK AT
                        from: thermal_acceptance.py --dry-run -> 'estimated wall time 160 s (2.7 min: 30 s ramp + 120 s window + 10 s allocation and first matmul)'. A WALL figure, and the only arm whose cost IS its window: it compiles nothing, times nothing and allocates two 8192^2 bf16 buffers once. Booked 3, above the figure and never at it. THE THREE TERMS ARE THE FLAGS ON THE ARM LINE, so a re-derivation runs the same command: --settle-seconds 30 is calibrate.settle_clocks' own budget for the governor's ramp (840 -> 1980 MHz on this card), --seconds 120 is four times the 2026-09-11 card's observed ~30 s to collapse AFTER that ramp, and --poll-seconds 2 puts 60 samples in the window against a floor of 9. The 10 s allocation term is an ALLOWANCE and the plan page says so: _load_compute's 256 MiB and cuBLAS's first-call kernel choice have not been timed here.
  calibrate           ~  3 min ALLOW   starts at ~3 min          This pod's own ridge and both dtype peaks. F
                        from: calibrate_hardware.py --dry-run prints NO time estimate: a bandwidth ladder, an 8192^3 GEMM per dtype and up to 30 s of settle under load. 3 min is this file's own standing allowance and the one figure here that is not read off a plan.
                        NOT IN THAT FIGURE: everything: this row is an allowance, not a figure off a plan
  pin_probe-n64-g1    ~  2 min ALLOW   starts at ~6 min          The S6a gate ('observed tile_block_m = none'
                        from: moe.bench.cli prints no plan off a GPU box (no framework span registers), so there is no figure to read. 2 min is one profile-cell census under a pin.
                        NOT IN THAT FIGURE: everything: this row is an allowance, not a figure off a plan
  
  TOTAL ~8 minutes (~0h 8m) of what the arms' own plans PRICE,
  of which ~0 are KERNEL minutes: arms whose plans exclude compiles and
  allocation in their own words, on rows marked NOT IN THAT FIGURE.
  
  BOOK ABOVE THAT AND NEVER AT IT, AND THIS IS THE NUMBER TO BOOK ABOVE:
  ~8 minutes (~0h 8m), the same table with those 0 KERNEL minutes
  multiplied by 235/100. That factor is the ONE wall-over-model ratio this
  repo has measured -- 127 s logged against 54 s modelled for mixtral_g1 on
  the s4 arm -- and it is the factor replicate_noise_floor.py already applies
  to the 120 minutes it books above. IT IS AN ILLUSTRATION OF THE SIZE OF
  THE GAP AND NOT AN ESTIMATE OF ANY ARM: one datum, from a small sweep, and a
  large sweep amortises its compiles over more timings than a small one does.
  No figure in the column above was multiplied by it, and 2 of the rows above
  print a NOT IN THAT FIGURE line naming what their own figure leaves out.
  
  The 'starts at' range is priced start to bounded start, and both are an
  EARLIEST start: a rental shorter than an arm's start does not buy that arm,
  or anything under it. Nothing in this file has a deadline or a timeout -- an
  arm that overruns is not cut short, it pushes everything below it down the
  column -- which is why the column is printed at all instead of one number
  for the whole session.
  
